# The Ledgerkit Journal Format

This document describes the plain-text journal format that Ledgerkit
actually parses today, as established by five clean-room evidence
topics: `journal-parsing`, `commodity-styling`, `tags`, `loader`, and
`compat-system`. Every factual claim below is traced to a frozen
assertion from one of those topics' snapshots (cited inline as
`<topic>@v1#<assertion-id>`). Where a cited assertion's own
comparison-classification was `partial` rather than `aligned` — meaning
the independent as-built reconstruction surfaced something the
assertion's text does not fully state, or vice versa — that is called
out explicitly rather than smoothed over, per this topic's own
drafting discipline.

A general caveat that applies throughout: a claim that Ledgerkit's
behaviour for some syntax is "compatible" with hledger reflects what
this project's own compatibility register currently asserts about
itself, most of it checked only by reading the code/docs or by a
same-session executable check, not always by an independently
dispatched comparison against the real hledger binary. Where the
underlying register entry's own verification tier matters, it is
noted.

## 1. Files

Ledgerkit loads plain-text journal files. A root file and any file it
pulls in via `include` must have one of two recognised extensions,
`.journal` or `.ledger` (checked case-insensitively); every other
extension — including hledger's own `.j` alias — is rejected with a
`ParseError` (loader@v1#loader-007). Multi-file journals are built by
textual inclusion, described in full in §8.

## 2. Transactions

### 2.1 Header syntax

A transaction begins with a non-indented header line matched by a
single regex that accepts the primary date, an optional secondary date
introduced by `=`, an optional status flag (`*` for cleared, `!` for
pending, absent for uncleared), an optional parenthesised code, a
lazily-matched description, and an optional `;`-introduced inline
comment (journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-001). The date portion
accepts `YYYY-MM-DD`, `YYYY/MM/DD`, `YYYY.MM.DD`, and year-omitted forms
such as `M/DD` or `MM-DD`, with optional leading zeros and separators
that need not match between the two components
(journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-001). For example,
`2024-01-05 * Salary` parses to a cleared transaction with description
"Salary", and `2024-02-20=2024-02-22 * desc` sets a primary date of
2024-02-20 and a secondary date of 2024-02-22
(journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-001). A trailing `=` with nothing
date-shaped after it is simply not matched by the secondary-date group,
so the primary date is used alone (journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-001).

### 2.2 Dates outside a header

Where a bare date needs parsing (for example inside a directive), it is
handled by a separate simple-date rule: an optional 4-digit year
followed by a 1-2 digit month and day separated by `-`, `/`, or `.`;
when the year is omitted, the journal's current default year is
substituted (itself changeable by a `Y` directive — see §7); an
impossible calendar date, such as month 13, is a hard `ParseError`
(journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-002).

### 2.3 Status, code, description, comments

The status flag and parenthesised code are both optional and are
captured directly by the header regex described in §2.1
(journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-001). Comment handling — both the
transaction's own inline comment and any indented follow-on comment
lines — is covered in full in §5, since the same taxonomy governs
comments on postings too.

## 3. Postings

### 3.1 Account/amount split

A posting line is split into an account part and an amount part on the
first run of **two or more** whitespace characters, not one
(journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-003). This is a deliberate choice:
it lets an account name that itself contains a single internal space
(e.g. `expenses:fun money`) survive intact, because only a run of 2+
spaces is treated as the account/amount delimiter
(journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-003). A posting line with only a
single space between what looks like an account and an amount is not
split there at all — the whole line, amount-looking text included,
becomes the account name (journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-003). If
nothing follows the split, the posting's amount is elided (`amount is
None`), to be resolved later (§3.2) (journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-003).

### 3.2 Elided amounts

At most one posting per transaction may have an elided amount; a second
elided posting in the same transaction block is rejected with a
`ParseError` at parse time (journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-005).
A separate function, `resolve_elision`, computes what an elided
posting's amount should be: if exactly one posting in the transaction
has no amount, it computes the net sum per commodity across all the
*other*, explicit postings, and replaces the elided posting with one
synthetic posting per commodity — each holding the negation of that
commodity's net, each flagged as inferred, each sorted by commodity
symbol, and each spliced in at the elided posting's original position
(journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-005; commodity-styling@v1#commodity-styling-007).
If there is no elided posting at all, the posting list is returned
unchanged; a two-or-more-elided case (only reachable by constructing a
`Transaction` directly, since normal parsing already rejects it) is
also returned unchanged by this function rather than erroring
(journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-005). For example, a transaction
with postings in £, $, and one elided posting resolves to two inferred
postings, in alphabetical commodity order ("$" before "£" by Unicode
code point, with "EUR" sorting before both) (commodity-styling@v1#commodity-styling-007).

**Resolution is not automatic.** `resolve_elision` is a separate,
opt-in post-processing step — a transaction's elided posting keeps its
`amount` as `None` until a caller explicitly invokes it; nothing in the
parser calls it on the parser's own behalf (journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-005,
classified `aligned` against an independent as-built reconstruction that
confirmed this live).

### 3.3 Balance assertions, cost, and lot annotations

A posting's amount token may additionally carry a balance assertion
(one of `=`, `==`, `=*`, `==*`, detected by a dedicated marker that
requires whitespace on both sides) and/or a cost annotation (`@
PRICE` for a unit cost or `@@ TOTAL` for a total cost, with `@@` checked
before `@`) and/or a lot annotation (`{AMOUNT}`, `{{AMOUNT}}`, `[DATE]`,
or `(LABEL)`) — and these are mutually composable on one posting
(journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-009). They are stripped and parsed
in a fixed order: the cost annotation first, then lot annotations, then
space-digit-group normalisation, then the main amount regex
(journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-009). For example, a quoted
commodity combined with a balance assertion (`3 "Frogs" = 30 "Frogs"`)
parses correctly to a posting amount of 3 Frogs with an assertion of 30
Frogs (journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-009).

**Confirmed limitation — balance-assignment elision syntax is not
implemented, and fails in two different ways.** hledger's own
"balance assignment" syntax writes an elided posting amount together
with a balance assertion as `account  = AMOUNT` (no separate amount
before the `=`). Ledgerkit's assertion-marker detector requires
whitespace *before* the marker by design, so a bare leading `=` is
never recognised as an assertion at all; the whole string, `=` included,
is instead handed to ordinary amount parsing
(journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-009). For a prefix-commodity amount
(e.g. `a  = $100`) this produces a clean, if unhelpfully-worded,
`ParseError: invalid amount: '= $100'`
(journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-009). This same assertion's
underlying evidence was found, under independent reconstruction, to be
incomplete on one point the assertion's own text generalises too far:
for a **suffix-commodity amount under comma-decimal mode** (e.g.
`a  = 100,00 EUR`), the attempt does not fail at all — it silently
produces a posting with `commodity` literally set to the single
character `=` and no balance assertion, because the prefix-symbol
amount regex has nothing excluding `=` from the set of characters it
will accept as a currency symbol. This specific failure mode is not
itself written into the frozen assertion text (which only generalises
"it simply fails amount parsing" from the prefix-commodity case), so
this topic's comparison review classified it `partial` rather than
`aligned` on exactly this point (journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-009).
In short: balance-assignment elision is unsupported, and depending on
commodity placement and decimal mode, attempting it either raises a
clear parse error or silently produces a wrong, uncaught result — both
are current, confirmed facts about the parser's behaviour, not merely a
documented deferral.

### 3.4 Posting-level date tag overrides

A posting's `date_override` and `date2_override` fields are set only
from the *first* occurrence of a `date:` or `date2:` comment tag found
in that **same posting's own** comment tags — never from the
transaction's own tags, and a transaction-level `date`/`date2` tag has
no override effect at all (journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-010;
tags@v1#tags-008). An unparseable date value in such a tag is a hard
error: raised immediately in strict parsing, or collected with the
override left unset in lenient parsing
(journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-010). The *effective* date used
when reading a posting back out follows a precedence chain: the
posting's own date override wins over its transaction's date for the
primary date; for the secondary date, the chain checks, in order, the
posting's own `date2` override, then the transaction's own secondary
date, then — distinctively — the posting's own *primary* date override
reused as a secondary-date fallback, then finally the transaction's
primary date (tags@v1#tags-008). This date-override mechanism is a
genuine, first-non-None-wins precedence chain, which stands in contrast
to how tag *values* themselves are combined (§6.2) — the two mechanisms
are unrelated (tags@v1#tags-008).

## 4. Amounts and commodities

### 4.1 Supported amount forms

Amount parsing accepts a commodity symbol as a prefix (`£30.00`) or a
suffix (`30.00 EUR`); a leading minus sign; and a "mid" minus sign that
appears after a prefix symbol (`$-300`)
(journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-004). By default, commas are
thousands separators and a period is the decimal mark; this reverses
(period-thousands, comma-decimal) once a `decimal-mark ,` directive is
active (§7) (journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-004). Space-separated
digit groups (`1 000 000 JPY`) and E-notation are also normalised
before the main amount pattern is applied
(journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-004). An amount with no commodity
symbol at all is a `ParseError` ("amount has no commodity symbol")
unless a default commodity has been set via a `D` directive
(journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-004).

### 4.2 How a commodity's display style is inferred

Separately from parsing an individual amount, Ledgerkit infers a
*display style* per commodity symbol — which side the symbol sits on,
whether a space separates it from the number, which character is the
decimal mark, which is the group separator, and how many decimal
digits to show. This inference looks at one sample raw-amount string
for the commodity and works as follows:

- **Symbol position.** The sample string (with any leading minus
  stripped first) is checked for whether it starts with the commodity
  symbol (prefix) or ends with it (suffix); if it happens to match
  both ends (a degenerate case, e.g. a symbol of `A` against `A5A`),
  the prefix check is tried first and wins
  (commodity-styling@v1#commodity-styling-001). If the symbol matches
  **neither** the start nor the end of the sample, this is not a benign
  no-op: the entire stripped string, including whatever unrelated text
  is actually present, is fed to decimal/group-separator inference as
  if it were the numeric portion, which can silently produce a
  nonsensical precision value — this has been directly confirmed by
  execution (e.g. inferring a style for commodity `EUR` from the
  sample `"100.00 USD"` yields a precision of 6, because the unrelated
  trailing `" USD"` text gets counted into the digit count), and no
  test in the project's own suite currently exercises this branch
  (commodity-styling@v1#commodity-styling-001).
- **Decimal mark vs. group separator, single-separator case.** If the
  numeric part contains only one kind of separator character (only
  dots, or only commas), and it occurs exactly once, is not at
  position 0, and is followed by exactly 3 digits, it is read as a
  digit-group separator (so the number is a whole integer with
  thousands grouping, precision 0); in every other single-separator
  case that same character is read as the decimal mark, with precision
  equal to the digit count after its last occurrence
  (commodity-styling@v1#commodity-styling-002). This heuristic is
  necessarily ambiguous for a genuinely-intended 3-decimal-place amount
  written with a single separator (e.g. `$1.234` meant as "one and 234
  thousandths" is indistinguishable from "1,234 grouped"), which the
  rule's own evidence acknowledges as an inherent limitation, not a
  bug to be fixed (commodity-styling@v1#commodity-styling-002).
- **Decimal mark vs. group separator, both-present case.** If the
  numeric part contains both a dot and a comma anywhere, whichever one
  occurs **rightmost** (closest to the end of the string) is always
  taken as the decimal mark and the other as the group separator —
  regardless of how many times either character repeats — and this
  check takes priority over the single-separator heuristic above
  (commodity-styling@v1#commodity-styling-003).

### 4.3 Formatting amounts for display

When formatting a `Decimal` value back into text for a given
commodity's style, the negative sign is placed differently depending on
symbol position, and this is an unconditional, hledger-convention
asymmetry rather than a simplification: for a prefix-style commodity,
the sign goes *immediately* after the symbol and before the number with
no gap at all — even if the style otherwise puts a space between symbol
and number for positive amounts, that gap is dropped entirely for a
negative one (e.g. `£-5,000.00`); for a suffix-style commodity, the
sign instead goes before the number, and the suffix symbol keeps its
normal spacing (e.g. `-1,234.56 EUR`) (commodity-styling@v1#commodity-styling-004).

### 4.4 Which sample wins when more than one is available

A journal can supply more than one sample amount for the same commodity
symbol — from an explicit `commodity` directive, from a posting amount,
and from a price (`P`) directive. Ledgerkit resolves one style per
commodity using a three-tier priority: an explicit `commodity`
directive's own sample amount always wins if one exists for that
symbol; otherwise, the first posting amount seen for that commodity (in
transaction/posting order) is used; otherwise, the first `P`-directive
amount for that commodity is used
(commodity-styling@v1#commodity-styling-005). This claim rests on
reading the implementation and its own docstring directly — no test in
the evidence available to this topic exercises the directive-overrides-
posting priority case end to end, so it is recorded here with that
caveat rather than as independently test-confirmed
(commodity-styling@v1#commodity-styling-005).

### 4.5 Overriding a commodity's style from the CLI

A commodity's display style can also be supplied directly (the CLI's
`-c`/`--commodity-style` flag), by giving a sample string such as
`"1,000.00 USD"` or `"$1,000.00"`; the symbol is extracted by first
trying to match a trailing alphabetic code, and only if that fails
trying a leading symbol, with the whole operation raising an error if
the string is empty, contains no digit at all, or contains digits but
matches neither pattern (e.g. a bare `"123.45"` with no commodity
symbol anywhere) (commodity-styling@v1#commodity-styling-006). Such an
override is scoped strictly to the one commodity symbol it names — it
has no effect on how any other commodity in the same report is
formatted (commodity-styling@v1#commodity-styling-006).

### 4.6 Known limitation in multi-commodity report formatting

One report-building code path — formatting the rows of a single report
section (as opposed to a whole journal) into a display table — has an
explicitly self-acknowledged, single-commodity limitation: it picks one
commodity (an arbitrary/first key out of the resolved per-commodity
style map) and applies that one style to *every* row, regardless of
which commodity each individual row's value actually came from; this
is called out directly in the code's own comments as a deferred
limitation, not merely an oversight nobody noticed
(commodity-styling@v1#commodity-styling-008). It stands in contrast to
whole-journal formatting elsewhere in the same codebase, which does
look up the correct per-commodity style for each row
(commodity-styling@v1#commodity-styling-008).

### 4.7 No multi-commodity ("mixed amount") postings

Ledgerkit's data model gives a posting a single, optional amount with a
single commodity — there is no concept analogous to hledger's "mixed
amount" on one posting (tags@v1#tags-009). Some of the tag-propagation
machinery (§6) is nonetheless written in a plural, list-based shape
specifically so that it would not need to change if this constraint
were ever lifted, even though, under the current data model, that
multi-commodity case cannot currently occur (tags@v1#tags-009).

## 5. Comments

Comment handling distinguishes four cases (journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-006):

1. A non-indented (column-0) line starting with `;` or `#` is always a
   silently-skipped top-level comment — it is never attached to any
   transaction or posting, regardless of what came immediately before
   it.
2. An indented line starting with `;` that appears inside an still-open
   transaction block is a follow-on comment: its text is appended
   (joined by a newline) to the *last-seen posting's* inline comment,
   or to the transaction's own inline comment if no posting has been
   seen yet in that block.
3. An indented line starting with `#` inside an open block extends the
   transaction's recorded source span, but its text is never attached
   to any comment field at all.
4. A same-line comment following `;` on a transaction header or
   posting line is captured directly as that line's own inline
   comment, by the same parsing step that reads the rest of the line.

Only `;`-led comments are ever scanned for tags (§6) — a `#`-led
comment, at any indentation, never contributes a tag
(journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-006). One deliberate edge case: a
standalone column-0 `;` line with no blank line separating it from a
preceding transaction is *not* treated as a follow-on comment on that
transaction's last posting — it is a top-level comment regardless of
adjacency (journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-006).

## 6. Tags

### 6.1 Tag syntax within a comment

A tag is written as `name:value` inside a comment; a comment line may
hold several, separated by commas (tags@v1#tags-005, tags@v1#tags-006).
Two rules govern this that are easy to get wrong by analogy to simpler
splitting:

- **A space immediately before the colon voids the entire candidate.**
  `"foo : bar"` yields *zero* tags — not a tag named `foo`, and not one
  named the empty string. Scanning resumes right after that voided
  colon, so a genuine tag appearing later on the same line is still
  found: `"foo : bar, real:tag"` yields exactly one tag, `real:tag`
  (tags@v1#tags-005).
- **A value runs to the next comma or end of line; a colon inside a
  value does not end it,** so `"url: http://example.com, other: x"`
  parses as two tags, `url` → `http://example.com` and `other` → `x`
  (tags@v1#tags-006). There is no escaping mechanism for a literal
  comma inside a value — a value can simply never contain one; this is
  treated as a real, permanent limitation of the grammar, not an
  omission to be fixed (tags@v1#tags-006).

Tag names have no character-class restriction beyond "not a colon, not
containing a line break, and not ending in whitespace before the
colon" — digits, punctuation, and non-ASCII characters are all accepted
as part of a tag name, which is more permissive than the hledger
manual's own prose describing tags as "a single word or hyphenated
word" (tags@v1#tags-007). The same tag name may legitimately appear
more than once on one line (or, by extension, across the sources
combined in §6.2) with different values; the result is always an
ordered list of `(name, value)` pairs, never collapsed into a
dictionary keyed by name (tags@v1#tags-007).

### 6.2 What contributes to a posting's effective tags

A posting's full, "effective" tag set is the union of exactly four
sources: the posting's own inline-comment tags; its transaction's own
inline-comment tags; any tags declared on the posting's account or any
ancestor of that account (via an `account` directive — see §7); and any
tags declared on the commodity used in the posting's amount (via a
`commodity` directive) (tags@v1#tags-001). Crucially, this union
performs **no shadowing, precedence, or deduplication of any kind** — a
tag named `rate` declared with four different values across all four
sources will appear four times in the result, not once; and this holds
even for two sources declaring the exact same `(name, value)` pair,
which is also preserved twice rather than collapsed (tags@v1#tags-002).
This is a deliberate design choice, documented as a reaction to the
observation that hledger's own manual prose ("posting tags override
account tags override commodity tags") does not match hledger's own
actual list-union-based mechanism either — but ledgerkit's own
implementation is "no dedup, ever," by direct inspection of its
concatenation logic, independent of what hledger's own internals are
believed to do (tags@v1#tags-002).

A second, narrower union exists specifically to back one query-matching
path (hledger's `accounts` command): it includes only the transaction's
own tags and the posting-account's inherited tags, explicitly excluding
the posting's own literal tags and any commodity-propagated tags that
the four-source union above would include (tags@v1#tags-003). Which
caller actually invokes this narrower union could not be independently
confirmed from this topic's own evidence and rests on the code's own
documentation of its purpose (tags@v1#tags-003).

### 6.3 Account tag inheritance

A declared account tag (from an `account` directive, §7) is inherited
by the declaring account itself and by every one of its descendants —
every prefix of the account's `:`-separated path, from the root segment
down to the account itself, is checked, and matching tags are
concatenated from least-specific to most-specific
(tags@v1#tags-004). A sibling branch does **not** inherit a tag
declared on a different sibling at a deeper level — only a true
ancestor (a strict prefix of the account's own path) contributes
(tags@v1#tags-004). For example, `assets:bank:savings` inherits tags
declared on both `assets` and `assets:bank`, but `assets:savings` (a
sibling of `assets:bank`, not a descendant of it) inherits only what
was declared on `assets` itself (tags@v1#tags-004).

## 7. Directives

Ledgerkit recognises the following non-indented directives
(journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-007):

- `account` — declares an account, optionally with its own tags.
- `commodity` — declares a commodity, with a sample amount used for
  style inference (§4.2) and optionally its own tags.
- `payee` — declares a payee name.
- `tag` — declares a tag name.
- `decimal-mark` — switches which of `,`/`.` is treated as the decimal
  mark for amounts parsed from that point forward (§4.1).
- `P` — records a market/commodity price on a given date.
- `alias` / `end aliases` — declares an account-name rewrite rule
  (exact, prefix, or regex-with-backreferences), applied in
  last-declared-first order until `end aliases` closes the scope.
- `Y` — sets the default year used when a date omits one (§2.2).
- `D` — sets a default commodity and style for amounts written with no
  commodity symbol at all (§4.1).
- `apply account` / `end apply account` — prefixes a directive-declared
  account scope onto subsequent postings.
- `comment` / `end comment` — brackets a block comment.

Two further headers are recognised but their bodies are deliberately
not implemented: a line beginning `~` (hledger's periodic transaction
rule) and a line beginning `=` at column 0 (hledger's auto-posting
rule) are both recognised, their following indented lines are skipped
rather than parsed as ordinary postings, and a `ParseWarning` (a
non-fatal subclass of parse error, collected rather than raised in
lenient mode) is emitted for each — this is a disclosed, deliberate
scope deferral rather than a bug (journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-007).

### 7.1 Strict vs. lenient parsing, and a confirmed exception

Ledgerkit exposes two parsing entry points with different error
policies: one raises on the first malformed line; the other is
documented and designed to never raise, instead collecting every error
into a returned list, discarding only the one malformed transaction,
and resuming at the next blank line or transaction header — intended
for callers such as an editor that re-parses on every keystroke
(journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-008). Every directive handler that
can raise an error is, by design, gated so that the "never raise"
caller instead gets the error collected — **except one, confirmed
exception**: a malformed `commodity` directive (an empty body, an
unterminated quoted symbol, or an otherwise-unparseable body) is not
guarded at all, and raises straight out of the "never raises" entry
point too, contradicting its own documented contract
(journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-008). This is a real, reproducible
parser bug, not a documentation nuance — it was confirmed by direct
execution during this topic's own review process, and no test in the
project's suite exercises this specific input shape
(journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-008).

A second, structurally different exception surfaced only during this
topic's independent as-built reconstruction, which this topic's own
comparison review folds into the same "except one" assertion as a
correction to its count rather than a contradiction of its thesis
(journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-008, classified `partial`): an
`alias` directive whose pattern compiles but whose replacement text
contains a backreference with no matching group (e.g. `alias /(foo)/ =
\2`) passes validation when the directive is declared, and only fails
later, when the alias is actually applied to an account name — and it
fails as an uncaught `re.PatternError` (not even a `ParseError`),
escaping both the strict and the "never raises" entry points alike.

### 7.2 Confirmed limitation — a directive inside an open transaction can silently drop postings

This topic's independent as-built reconstruction additionally found,
and this topic's comparison review records as a documented gap in
assertion journal-parsing-007's own directive inventory rather than a
contradiction of it (journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-007, classified
`partial`): if an `account`, `commodity`, `payee`, or `tag` directive
line appears between two posting lines of a transaction block that is
still open, the still-open transaction is never flushed or closed —
unlike what happens for a new transaction header, a periodic/
auto-posting rule header, or a `comment` block, each of which does
correctly close out any open transaction first. Instead, every
subsequent indented line of that same block (including what were meant
to be further posting lines) is silently treated as if it were a
sub-directive continuation line and dropped entirely — with no
`ParseError`, no `ParseWarning`, nothing. This is a genuine,
observable, silent-data-loss gap: a transaction can lose some of its
own postings with no indication anything went wrong, purely because an
unrelated directive happened to be written between two of its posting
lines.

## 8. Multi-file includes

### 8.1 The `include` directive

A line anchored at column 0, consisting of the literal word `include`
followed by one or more whitespace characters and then a target path
captured verbatim, triggers multi-file loading (loader@v1#loader-001).
An indented `include` line is never recognised as a directive at all —
the column-0 anchor means it is simply left as ordinary (and likely
invalid) content (loader@v1#loader-001). If the captured target, once
whitespace-trimmed, is empty, a `ParseError` ("include: missing file
path") is raised (loader@v1#loader-001).

### 8.2 How an include target is resolved

The target path is resolved by a fixed order of checks: a path
beginning with `~` is expanded against the user's home directory;
otherwise an absolute path is used as-is; otherwise the path is
resolved relative to the directory of the file that *contains* the
`include` line — which, for a chain of nested includes, is the
immediate parent file's own directory, not the root file's directory
and not the process's current working directory
(loader@v1#loader-002). If the resulting path contains a glob
metacharacter (`*`, `?`, or `[`), it is expanded as a glob pattern
(including `**` for recursive matching), and the matches are sorted for
a deterministic order; otherwise it is treated as a single literal
path (loader@v1#loader-002).

**Confirmed limitation — glob metacharacters cannot be escaped, and
this can misfire two different ways.** Because the glob-vs-literal test
is a simple character check with no escaping syntax, a filename that
genuinely contains `[`, `*`, or `?` as a literal character can be
silently and wrongly reclassified. One confirmed case: when a plain,
non-glob include target is resolved inside a *containing directory*
whose own name happens to contain a bracket character, the resolution
logic classifies the resulting joined path as a glob, `glob.glob` finds
zero matches even though the target file genuinely exists, and — because
the separate decision of "was the raw target a glob" (used to choose
between the two documented error types, see §8.4) was computed from the
raw target text alone and says "no" — the code takes a branch that ends
up indexing into an empty list, raising an uncaught `IndexError` instead
of either of the two error types the include-resolution contract
otherwise promises (loader@v1#loader-002, loader@v1#loader-003,
loader@v1#loader-007). This exact scenario was confirmed by direct
execution during this topic's own review, and the project's test suite
has no test for it; whether this is an accepted limitation or a bug to
fix was left as an open design question rather than guessed at
(loader@v1#loader-002, loader@v1#loader-003, loader@v1#loader-007). A
related but distinct case — a literal *filename* (rather than a
containing-directory name) containing a bracket — was separately found,
independently, during this topic's as-built reconstruction: it is
classified as a glob because the bracket is present in the raw target
text itself, so the attempt fails instead with a `ParseError: no files
matched` rather than an `IndexError`; because this is a different
reproduction than the containing-directory case, this topic's
comparison review records the three affected assertions as `partial`
rather than treating the two findings as confirming exactly the same
scenario (loader@v1#loader-002, loader@v1#loader-003, loader@v1#loader-007).

### 8.3 Self-exclusion and circular-include detection

Whenever an include target resolves to one or more files, the file
that contains the `include` directive is always filtered out of that
resolved list first, before anything else happens — this applies
whether the match came from a glob or from a single literal path
(loader@v1#loader-003). **Confirmed limitation:** this produces a
misleading error for a literal self-include. If a file contains
`include <its-own-filename>`, the self-filter empties the resolved
target list, and because the raw target was not a glob pattern, the
code reports `FileNotFoundError: include: file not found: <path>` for
a path that unquestionably exists on disk — the true cause (the file
excluding itself) is not what the error says. This was confirmed by
direct execution; no test in the project's suite covers it, and whether
a clearer, dedicated error message is warranted was left as an open
question rather than resolved here (loader@v1#loader-003).

Separately, circular includes are detected via an ancestor-chain set of
"currently in progress" absolute paths, threaded through the recursive
expansion: a path is added immediately before recursing into it and
removed immediately after that recursive call returns, so the set
tracks only the current chain of ancestors, not every file ever
processed (loader@v1#loader-004). A genuine mutual cycle (file A
includes file B, which includes file A) is correctly caught and raises
a `ParseError` naming it as circular (loader@v1#loader-004). **Confirmed
limitation — "diamond" include graphs are not caught, and produce
silent duplication.** If a root file includes two sibling files that
each, independently, include the same third file, this is not a cycle
under the ancestor-chain design — the shared file is no longer "in
progress" by the time the second sibling branch reaches it — so it
loads successfully, but the shared file's content (transactions,
declared items, everything) is duplicated in the result, with no
warning of any kind. This was confirmed by direct execution: a
three-file diamond produced five transactions in total (one shared
transaction appearing twice) with no error raised. Whether a
non-circular repeated inclusion like this should be allowed to succeed
silently, or should itself be flagged, was left as an open design
question rather than resolved here (loader@v1#loader-004).

### 8.4 Missing-file errors

If a target string was itself detected as containing a glob
metacharacter and resolution yields no matches, the result is a
`ParseError` ("no files matched"); if the target was not a glob and
resolution yields nothing, the result is instead a `FileNotFoundError`
naming the resolved absolute path and the referencing file/line — not a
`ParseError` (loader@v1#loader-007). This two-outcome description is
not exhaustive, however: as described in §8.2, a specific containing-
directory-name edge case produces a third, undocumented outcome (an
uncaught `IndexError`) instead of either of these two — which is why
this particular assertion's own evidentiary status was downgraded by
this topic's own review from a settled rule to a still-`proposed`
description pending a decision on whether that third outcome is
accepted or should be fixed (loader@v1#loader-007). Independently of
missing-file handling, an unsupported file extension, or a target
written with a recognised non-journal format prefix (such as `csv:` or
`timedot:`), is always rejected with a `ParseError` before the
not-found logic can even apply (loader@v1#loader-007).

### 8.5 How included content is merged, and error attribution

Multi-file loading works by **textual substitution, not an
object-level merge**: each `include` line is recursively replaced by
the full, already-expanded text of the file(s) it names, producing one
single combined text string that is parsed exactly once
(loader@v1#loader-005). A direct, intended consequence is that
directive scope active before an `include` line — the documented
example being an active `alias` — propagates naturally into the
included content, because the included text is spliced in at the exact
point of the directive within one continuous parse
(loader@v1#loader-005). A three-level nested include demonstrates this
is genuine positional splicing, not an append-at-the-end or any other
reordering: the innermost file's content appears first in the result,
ahead of its including file's own content, which appears ahead of the
root's own content (loader@v1#loader-005).

Because everything is parsed as one flattened blob, a parse error's
raw line number is relative to that flattened text, not to any original
file. To correct for this, the loader maintains a mapping with exactly
one entry per *retained* line of the expanded output (an `include` line
itself contributes no entry, since it is replaced rather than kept),
and when a `ParseError` is caught after parsing, it is re-raised with
the corrected originating file and local line number substituted in —
this remap applies only when the caught error's line number falls
within the range the map covers; outside that range, the original,
untranslated error propagates unchanged (loader@v1#loader-008).

### 8.6 Merging already-parsed journals

A separate, distinct mechanism exists for merging journals that have
already been independently parsed: it concatenates, in input order,
the transactions, prices, and all four kinds of declared items
(accounts, commodities, payees, tags) across every input, takes its
`source_file` from the *first* input only, and sums every input's own
included-file count into the result; a single-journal input is a fast
path that returns that exact object (identity, not a copy), and an
empty input list raises `ValueError` (loader@v1#loader-006). Because
this mechanism operates on already-parsed objects rather than on text,
it is architecturally distinct from `include`-based splicing: any
directive scoping captured while parsing one of the merged journals
does not and cannot propagate into another journal being merged this
way (loader@v1#loader-005).

## 9. Where Ledgerkit's handling is deliberately scoped differently from hledger's documented format

The following are confirmed, deliberate scope boundaries, not bugs:

- **Periodic transaction rules (`~`) and auto-posting rules (`=` at
  column 0) are recognised but not implemented.** Their header lines
  are detected, their bodies are skipped rather than parsed as
  ordinary postings, and a non-fatal warning is emitted for each — this
  is a disclosed deferral stated directly in the parser's own code
  (journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-007).
- **Balance-assignment elision syntax (`account  = AMOUNT` with no
  separate amount before the assertion) is not implemented** — see §3.3
  for the two different, confirmed ways an attempt to use it currently
  fails (journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-009).
- **There is no multi-commodity ("mixed amount") posting.** A posting
  holds at most one commodity in its amount; see §4.7
  (tags@v1#tags-009).

Separately, this project maintains a compatibility register that
tracks, per hledger feature, whether Ledgerkit's handling is considered
compatible, an intentional divergence, or explicitly unsupported. A few
register entries bear directly on journal-format syntax covered above,
with the following confidence levels as currently recorded:

- An entry claiming the `account` directive (§7) behaves equivalently
  to hledger's cites a real, existing test, but as of this survey that
  entry's own evidence was still only a documentation citation (not yet
  an executable comparison) — i.e. its claim has not yet been checked
  against a running hledger (compat-system@v1#compat-system-006).
- An entry claiming that commodity-directive comment tags (§6, §7)
  propagate to query matching identically to hledger has been checked
  by an independently-dispatched verifier against real command output,
  not merely read from the code (compat-system@v1#compat-system-007).
- An entry claiming posting-level `date`/`date2` tag override
  precedence (§3.4) matches hledger's own behaviour exactly has been
  checked by the same engineer/session that implemented the feature,
  with real command output, but not yet by a separately-dispatched
  verifier (compat-system@v1#compat-system-011).

This project's register also separately lists several hledger features
by name as explicitly unsupported in the live register's current
contents (as of this survey, 6 of 49 register entries carry this
classification) — this document does not repeat their individual
rationale beyond what is independently confirmed above (the periodic/
auto-posting deferral and the lack of multi-commodity postings), since
reading each entry's own stated reasoning in full was outside this
drafting pass's permitted evidence (compat-system@v1#compat-system-003).

## 10. A note on evidentiary confidence in this document

Several claims above are flagged inline as resting on reconstruction-
only findings that a cited assertion's own text does not fully state,
or as resting on evidence this topic's own review marked
`partially_supported` rather than fully test-confirmed. These are not
incidental hedges: per this clean-room workflow's own stated discipline,
agreement between a frozen assertion and an independent as-built
reconstruction ("aligned") means only that the *current* implementation
matches what was asserted — it does not, by itself, establish that the
asserted rule is the *correct* one, and a `partial` classification means
one of the two artefacts said something specific the other did not
corroborate. Nothing in this document should be read as a guarantee
that behaves identically to hledger's own documented format beyond what
is stated explicitly, with its stated confidence, above.
